<h1 id="installation">Installation</h1>
<p>Install Jundot, start the editor, and connect an AI backend before using the AI-assisted project workflow.</p>

<h2 id="download">Download</h2>
<ol>
    <li>Download the Jundot package for your operating system.</li>
    <li>Extract it into a stable folder outside temporary download directories.</li>
    <li>Start the Jundot editor executable.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="first-launch">First launch</h2>
<p>On first launch, create or import a project. You can also use the AI project creation panel to describe a game idea and let the AI propose the starting project structure.</p>

<h2 id="ai-backend">AI backend</h2>
<p>Open AI settings and configure the backend used by Jundot AI. The assistant needs a working backend before it can create projects, inspect code, fix errors, or prepare GitHub updates.</p>

<h2 id="project-location">Project location</h2>
<p>Use a short, stable local path for projects. Avoid paths inside cloud sync folders while the AI is writing files, because external sync tools can lock files during edits.</p>

<h2 id="github">GitHub access</h2>
<p>If you want the AI to upload verified changes, set up Git and GitHub access before starting. The user should review the change and run the project before allowing upload.</p>

<h2 id="verify">Verify the setup</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Create a small test project.</li>
    <li>Ask the AI to inspect the project and summarize the current scene.</li>
    <li>Run the project once from the editor.</li>
    <li>Confirm that Git status works if you plan to use GitHub upload.</li>
</ul>
